#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510844} {:answer/id #uuid "32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510890} {:answer/id #uuid "7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511041} {:answer/id #uuid "878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511062} {:answer/id #uuid "89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510844} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36799" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y &=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$"}]}
